# XSH timer_delete()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Deleting a timer whose ID is not valid is undefined, so only valid deletions are tested. What
# happens to a signal already pending for the deleted timer is unspecified and not tested.

assertion  XSH.timer_delete.001
anchor     functions/timer_delete.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/timer_delete.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       timer_delete() of a timer that was created and never armed returns 0.
test       t/001-returns-zero.c

assertion  XSH.timer_delete.002
anchor     functions/timer_delete.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       An armed timer (200 ms) that is deleted before it expires:
           timer_delete() returns 0 and no expiration signal is accepted within
           600 ms.
note       The timer is deleted well before its first expiration, so no pending
           signal can be left for it.
test       t/002-armed-disarmed.c

assertion  XSH.timer_delete.003
anchor     functions/timer_delete.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/timer_create.html#ERRORS/EAGAIN
class      required
since      2001
says       Creating and deleting a timer 2000 times in a row never fails: each
           deleted timer's resources are available again.
note       Rests on the limit being on timers that exist (the timer ID is unique
           until the timer is deleted; the per-process limit is on timers created
           and not deleted).
test       t/003-resources-freed.c
